\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Evidence-family separation for the adaptive G-CVaR evaluation. The diagram is a reporting map: it distinguishes the roles of the primary, supplemental, and governance evidence without implying that results from one family validate another.}}{19}{figure.bodyfig.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Implementation-audit view of the G-CVaR protocol. The checks document correspondence between the stated methodology and recorded implementation fields; they do not, by themselves, establish investment performance.}}{20}{figure.bodyfig.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces End-to-end interaction flow from user query to governed response. The LLM planner classifies intent, selects and sequences the appropriate specialised agents, evaluates their results, and assembles a compliance-checked, human-friendly response. Memory context from prior turns is injected at the planner stage.}}{22}{figure.bodyfig.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Portfolio state variables computed by the DiagnosticsAgent. The agent computes concentration metrics (HHI, EffN), sector exposures, profit-and-loss attribution, rolling volatility, mean pairwise correlation, and conditional value-at-risk estimates from the current portfolio weights and live market data.}}{24}{figure.bodyfig.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Controlled HITL simulation governance loop. The figure shows the review cycle in which the explainability layer presents a recommendation, evidence bundle, and fidelity signals to the simulated reviewer; the reviewer may approve the allocation, request clarification, modify constraints, or reject the proposal; and the orchestrator re-runs the relevant analytical pipeline until an acceptable governed decision is reached and logged for execution.}}{28}{figure.bodyfig.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Proposed framework architecture, showing the complete multi-layer pipeline spanning data ingestion, instability detection, deterministic switching, and conversational explainability under a unified central orchestrator.}}{29}{figure.bodyfig.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Agent task decomposition and module interfaces. Each agent encapsulates a single formally defined computational task and communicates through an immutable data contract. Agent\nobreakspace {}A7 (LLM Interface) has strictly read-only access to the committed governance record $\mathcal {G}_t$ and no upstream write path.}}{30}{figure.bodyfig.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Orchestrator execution flowchart across the seven-agent pipeline. The blue path executes agents sequentially during session initialisation. The orange path routes chat queries through intent classification before calling the read-only LLM interface. The teal path triggers selective agent re-execution when parameters change. Red arrows indicate shared exception handling.}}{31}{figure.bodyfig.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Observed terminal-value and CVaR trade-off across the compared objective formulations. The figure describes the realised experimental frontier and should not be read as evidence of universal dominance or a causal effect.}}{38}{figure.bodyfig.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Time-varying graph exposure under the calibrated walk-forward protocol. Variation is interpreted as an observed response under the frozen threshold and gate settings, not as proof that graph exposure alone caused subsequent outcomes.}}{38}{figure.bodyfig.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Analytical attribution snapshot showing how explanatory evidence can be linked to portfolio diagnostics and recommendation logic.}}{42}{figure.bodyfig.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Analytical decision trace from portfolio input to governed recommendation output.}}{45}{figure.bodyfig.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Five-agent blackboard pipeline illustrating the deterministic supervisory workflow, agent boundaries, and governance logging structure.}}{52}{figure.bodyfig.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Grouped validation summary supporting the modular planner-agent design of the prototype.}}{53}{figure.bodyfig.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Orchestrator execution flowchart showing session start, intent routing, parameter-change handling, and selective agent re-execution.}}{57}{figure.bodyfig.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Execution trace used to assess runtime behaviour and coordination overhead in the modular architecture.}}{59}{figure.bodyfig.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Universe-level CVaR-loss and maximum-drawdown changes relative to Equal Weight. Mixed comparisons contextualise downside outcomes rather than establish uniform dominance.}}{68}{figure.bodyfig.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Share of the eleven universes in which Adaptive G-CVaR is the best strategy in the complete-metric comparison. The low shares rule out a universal-superiority interpretation even though the primary three-strategy protocol shows frequent return and Sharpe wins.}}{70}{figure.bodyfig.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19}{\ignorespaces Adaptive G-CVaR rank by universe and metric in the complete-metric comparison; lower values are better.}}{70}{figure.bodyfig.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {20}{\ignorespaces Universe 1 performance diagnostics and stress overlay, read jointly to relate realised outcomes to stress timing.}}{70}{figure.bodyfig.20}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {21}{\ignorespaces Universe 2 performance diagnostics and stress overlay, retaining the universe-specific risk and return pattern.}}{71}{figure.bodyfig.21}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {22}{\ignorespaces Validation and untouched-test diagnostics for the authoritative adaptive gate.}}{74}{figure.bodyfig.22}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {23}{\ignorespaces Crisis-only governance comparison. This restricted sample provides conditional mechanism evidence, not an unconditional performance estimate.}}{74}{figure.bodyfig.23}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {24}{\ignorespaces Graph-CVaR risk-adjustment frontier aggregated across the eleven universes.}}{76}{figure.bodyfig.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Aggregate cumulative performance comparison (log scale) across the eleven universes. Source: completed 2014--2025 analysis run.}}{76}{figure.bodyfig.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {26}{\ignorespaces Bootstrap Sharpe ratio confidence intervals (95\% limits) across strategy families. Source: completed 2014--2025 analysis run.}}{77}{figure.bodyfig.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {27}{\ignorespaces Monthly return distributions for Standard CVaR versus Adaptive G-CVaR. Source: completed 2014--2025 analysis run.}}{77}{figure.bodyfig.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {28}{\ignorespaces Incremental component contribution study showing the Sharpe ratio waterfall. Source: completed 2014--2025 analysis run.}}{78}{figure.bodyfig.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {29}{\ignorespaces Paired ablation evidence for incremental Sharpe contribution and composite-governance score changes under the specified sequence.}}{79}{figure.bodyfig.29}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {30}{\ignorespaces Risk-return governance map highlighting the trade-offs of portfolio optimisation strategies. Source: completed 2014--2025 analysis run.}}{79}{figure.bodyfig.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces Simulated HITL terminal-value comparison for the documented \$100,000 sample; the scenario-specific result does not imply realised investor benefit.}}{79}{figure.bodyfig.31}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces Simulated HITL decisions, event impact, and ticker network risk versus adopted allocation; the panels document rule behaviour within the sample only.}}{80}{figure.bodyfig.32}%
